{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 15,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.74,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Quadrilaterals"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.086,
            0.847,
            0.109
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Deduction 6— What can we say about the angles of a parallelogram?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.117,
            0.823,
            0.138
          ],
          "content": "In the parallelogram ABCD, $\\mathrm { A B }   |   |   \\mathrm { C D } |$ , and AD is a transversal to them."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.147,
            0.497,
            0.203
          ],
          "content": "$\\angle \\mathrm{A} + \\angle \\mathrm{D} = 180^{\\circ}$ (sum of the internal angles on the same side of a transversal)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.203,
            0.284,
            0.22
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.221,
            0.461,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{D}=180-\\angle \\mathrm{A}=180-30=150^{\\circ}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.24,
            0.503,
            0.277
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, $\\mathrm { A D }   |   |   \\mathrm { B C } ,$ and AB and CD are transversals to them."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.507,
            0.142,
            0.931,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.415,
            0.287,
            0.606,
            0.307
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{S}0,\\angle \\mathrm{A}+\\angle \\mathrm{B}=180^{\\circ}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.415,
            0.317,
            0.607,
            0.336
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{S}0,\\angle \\mathrm{C}+\\angle \\mathrm{D}=180^{\\circ}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.34,
            0.673,
            0.362
          ],
          "content": "Using these equations, we get $\\angle \\mathrm{B} = 150^{\\circ}$ and $\\angle C = 30^{\\circ}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.37,
            0.835,
            0.409
          ],
          "content": "We see that in this parallelogram, the **adjacent pairs of angles add up to 180° and opposite pairs of angles are equal.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.409,
            0.236,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": "Thus,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.431,
            0.828,
            0.452
          ],
          "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{A}+\\angle \\mathrm{B}=180^{\\circ},\\angle \\mathrm{A}+\\angle \\mathrm{D}=180^{\\circ},\\angle \\mathrm{C}+\\angle \\mathrm{D}=180^{\\circ},\\mathrm{and}\\angle \\mathrm{B}+\\angle \\mathrm{C}=180^{\\circ}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.456,
            0.204,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": "And,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.386,
            0.48,
            0.612,
            0.499
          ],
          "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{A} = \\angle \\mathrm{C}, \\mathrm{and} \\angle \\mathrm{B} = \\angle \\mathrm{D}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.515,
            0.838,
            0.554
          ],
          "content": "Since the adjacent angles are the interior angles on the same side of a transversal to a pair of parallel lines, they must add up to 180°."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.556,
            0.847,
            0.578
          ],
          "content": "? What about the opposite angles? Will they be equal in all parallelograms?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.576,
            0.424,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "If yes, how can we be sure?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.599,
            0.505,
            0.618
          ],
          "content": "Let us take one of the angles to be x."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.618,
            0.421,
            0.636
          ],
          "content": "What are the other angles?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.636,
            0.362,
            0.654
          ],
          "content": "Since $\\angle \\mathrm{P} + \\angle \\mathrm{R} = 180^{\\circ}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.655,
            0.385,
            0.672
          ],
          "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{R} = 180 - \\angle \\mathrm{P} = 180 - x.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_23.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.673,
            0.458,
            0.691
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, since   \\angle \\mathrm{A} + \\angle \\mathrm{R} = 180^{\\circ},",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_24.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.692,
            0.605,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": "\\angle A = 180 - \\angle R = 180 - (180 - x) = 180 - 180 + x = x.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_25.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.711,
            0.332,
            0.728
          ],
          "content": "Thus, $\\angle \\mathrm{P} = \\angle \\mathrm{A} = x.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.608,
            0.583,
            0.899,
            0.726
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_27.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.729,
            0.624,
            0.747
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, we can deduce that $\\angle \\mathrm{R} = \\angle \\mathrm{E} = 180 - x.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.747,
            0.847,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, this shows that **the opposite angles of a parallelogram are always equal.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.794,
            0.845,
            0.816
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Deduction 7— What can we say about the sides of a parallelogram?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.825,
            0.848,
            0.862
          ],
          "content": "By looking at a parallelogram, it appears that the opposite sides are equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.862,
            0.848,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": "Can we again use congruence to show this? Which two triangles can be considered for this?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.877,
            0.915,
            0.912,
            0.936
          ],
          "content": "97"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
